# Adaptive Layout Standard (build for every screen size, always)

Bridger is one Expo codebase that runs on iOS, Android, and web, and must be
ready for big folding phones (iPhone Duo), tablets/iPad, and desktop browsers.
**No UI is "done" if it only looks right on a phone.** Build screen-size
adaptation into every feature as you go, never as a later pass.

## The one source of truth

- Use **`useResponsiveLayout()`** from `@bridger/ui`
  (`packages/ui/src/layout/responsive.ts`). It returns live `width`/`height`, a
  `breakpoint` (`phone | expanded | large`), `isLarge`, `isPortrait`, and the
  `contentMaxWidth` to cap at.
- Never hard-code a device width, and never read `Dimensions.get()` once at
  module load (it will not update on rotate, fold, or window resize). Branch on
  `breakpoint` / `isLarge` instead.
- Tune sizing through `LAYOUT` in that file (e.g. `CONTENT_MAX_WIDTH`). Change it
  there so the whole app moves together.

## What to check on every UI change

1. **Comfortable middle, not stretched.** Content and nav sit in a centered
   column on big screens (handled by `Screen` + `FloatingTabBar`). If you build
   a screen outside that scaffold, cap and center it with `contentMaxWidth`
   yourself.
2. **Use the room on big screens.** On `expanded` / `large`, prefer the planned
   two-column / master-detail layouts over a single stretched strip
   (`guide-docs/design-briefs/FOLDABLE-DUO-ADAPTIVE-DESIGN-BRIEF.md`).
3. **Reflow, do not overflow.** Test narrow phone, unfolded foldable, iPad, a
   resized split-view window, and a wide browser. Nothing clips, no fixed pixel
   widths force horizontal scroll, images and cards scale.
4. **Both orientations + fold posture.** Handle portrait and landscape; keep key
   text/media out of the center-fold gutter when `isPortrait` book posture
   applies.
5. **Web too.** The same component must behave on web (mouse, keyboard focus,
   window resize), not just native.
6. **Accessibility holds at every size.** Tap targets stay 44x44 or larger and
   Dynamic Type / font scaling never breaks the layout when the screen changes.

## Example

```tsx
// ❌ BAD: locks to a phone width, stretches on web/foldable, never updates.
const W = Dimensions.get('window').width;
<View style={{ width: W }}>...</View>

// ✅ GOOD: adapts live to phone / foldable / tablet / web.
const { isLarge, contentMaxWidth } = useResponsiveLayout();
<View style={{ width: '100%', maxWidth: contentMaxWidth, alignSelf: 'center' }}>
  {isLarge ? <TwoPaneLayout /> : <SingleColumn />}
</View>
```

If a needed adaptive primitive (e.g. `TwoPane`, side nav rail) does not exist
yet, build it in `packages/ui/src/layout/` and reuse it — do not hand-roll a
one-off in a screen.
